Spawn, objective 239 [performance] (owner request t517u, 2026-11-19 21:19 +08; owner contract by lead-mac; design: docs/fleet-architecture.md §3.2 "Work stealing" and docs/design.md §14, both in this contract): work stealing. An idle resident of a pool takes one unstarted task from a busy peer's queue, by a recorded move under the append lock; a started task never moves.

Why. Each resident works one task at a time and its next tasks wait in storage (`Work::blocked`, `next_up`). A resident whose task is delivered, under review, or accepted and waiting for the gate holds the rest of its queue: on live, 48 of mission 4's 139 resident tasks waited more than a minute for session_resident_rsi, the longest 1,055 minutes (17.6 h), and 10 of mission 6's waited up to 434 minutes. Nothing today lets a second, idle worker take queued work: `spawn task reassign` takes only a delivered task and makes a fresh ephemeral worker. The owner's example: A is idle; B runs one task and has two unstarted log-analysis tasks queued; A takes one of them, B keeps the rest, and the running task stays B's.

What exists and stays: the per-worker queue is `mission_tasks` folded from the control stream, as delivery and the stall rules read it (`deliver::Work::blocked`, `Turns`, `next_up`); there is no second queue. Restart, reassign, re-issue, cancel, pause and the closure rule are unchanged.

What changes (every rule is the design section's; read it first):

1. Pools. `spawn resident propose --name N --charter C [--pool P]` (P: the resident name pattern, ^[a-z][a-z0-9_]{0,30}$). `resident.proposed` names `pool` (absent or null without one); `mission_residents.pool` (text, null by default) folded from it, refold included. A resident is in its pool once approved. Without a pool it neither takes nor gives a task. No pool change after the proposal (retire and propose again).

2. Pins. `spawn task assign ... [--pin]`: `task.assigned` names `pinned` true (absent otherwise); `mission_tasks.pinned` boolean not null default false, folded. The supervisor's tool and the gateway pass it through as they pass `--model`.

3. The move: the store's `spawn_task_steal(p_task bigint, p_to text /* resident name */, p_by text, p_reason text) returns jsonb`, granted to the dispatcher's login and the owner's, search_path pinned, closed to public, as every mission function. Under the append lock (pg_advisory_xact_lock(1399957335)) it checks, in this order, and refuses with an exception whose message starts `task <id> ` and names the first rule that fails, recording nothing:
   - the task exists, is `kind` resident, state `assigned`, `revisions` 0, and no `spawn_commands` row has an idempotency key `deliver-<id>-` followed by anything: else "task <id> is not queued (<state> | revised | a delivery command exists | not a resident task)";
   - not pinned: else "task <id> is pinned to <worker name>";
   - its mission open (not closed), not paused, not halted: else "task <id> waits for its mission <mid> (closed | paused | halted)";
   - the taker `p_to` is an approved resident (not proposed, not retired) and not the task's worker: else "task <id> cannot go to <name> (not approved | retired | already its worker | no such resident)";
   - the task's worker and the taker both have a pool and it is the same pool: else "task <id> cannot go to <name> (no pool | pool <P> is not <Q>)";
   - the taker is idle: no task of its own `assigned`, `delivered`, `reported`, `revise`, `landing`, or `accepted` in a mission with a gate, and no cancel notice not yet told (a delivered task of its, cancelled as its latest delivery, whose `cancel-notice-<task>-<n>` command has not finished taken): else "task <id> cannot go to <name> (not idle: task <k> <state> | a cancel notice is not told)";
   - the task is in its worker's queue, never the one delivery gives its worker now (the design's rule, deliver::Work::blocked and next_up): the worker holds a task `delivered`, `reported`, `revise`, `landing`, or `accepted` in a mission with a gate, or a cancel notice not yet told, or an older task of its own in `assigned`. A worker's own `assigned` tasks do not make it busy, so an idle worker's oldest task is always refused: else "task <id> is its worker's next (its worker is idle)". After a move this is what refuses a second taker: the task's new worker holds nothing else.
   On success it appends `task.stolen` {id, from_session, from_agent, to_session, to_agent, pool, by, reason (null when none), queued_since_ms: the task's created_at_ms} and folds `session_id`, `agent_id` onto the task (updated_at_ms the record's time). It returns {task, from_session, to_session, agent_id}. Nothing else changes on the task.

4. `spawn_task_delivered(task, command)` refuses (returns false, or raises, as it refuses a state today) when the command's session is not the task's current `session_id`: a delivery planned before a move is never recorded for the old worker.

5. The dispatcher: in each pass, before it plans deliveries (deliver::orders), unless SPAWN_WORK_STEALING is 0, for each pool: its idle residents (the store's idle rule, read from the pass's Work) in name order each take one task, the pool's oldest stealable task (lowest id, from any other member's queue), through spawn_task_steal(..., 'spawn-dispatch', 'oldest queued task of a busy pool member'). A refusal (a race lost) is one line in the loop's log and nothing else; the pass then reads Work again before planning deliveries. No model call, no judge, no digest item, no new polled read: the candidates come from the Work the pass already read, plus the store's one call per move.

6. The owner's move: `spawn task steal --id N --to <resident> [--reason <text>]` (owner login; by `human`) prints the store's answer, or exits 1 with "spawn: task steal: <the store's refusal>".

7. Seen: `spawn task stages --id N` shows a stage `stolen` {at_ms, from (session), to (session), by} where the record falls; `GET /api/missions` shows the task under its new session (it reads the folded row). The delivery stall of a moved task is measured from its task.stolen (the record that made it ready for its new worker).

Files: crates/spawn-postgres/sql/schema.sql (mission_residents.pool, mission_tasks.pinned, the folds of resident.proposed, task.assigned and task.stolen, spawn_task_steal, spawn_task_delivered's session check, grants), crates/spawn-postgres/src/missions.rs, crates/spawn-cli/src/missions.rs (the three flags, `task steal`), crates/spawn-cli/src/deliver.rs or a new crates/spawn-cli/src/dispatch/steal.rs (the pass's rule), crates/spawn-cli/src/dispatch.rs (call it before orders), the supervisor tool's assign if it builds the CLI line. Tests: the store's refusals, each idle state of the taker (assigned, delivered, reported, revise, landing, accepted in a gated mission, an untold cancel notice) and the worker's-next rule, the two-takers race, and spawn_task_delivered refusing a command into a session that is not the task's (crates/spawn-postgres/tests), the dispatcher's choice (oldest, one per idle resident, name order, off with SPAWN_WORK_STEALING=0). Keep tests to these behaviours. Owner: rsi/scenarios/239.sh, rsi/backlog/239.json, the two design sections.

Not in scope: ephemeral workers (one task each: no queue), moving delivered or sent-back work, stealing across pools, any change to reassign, restart, re-issue, gates, alerts, or the supervisor's charter text.

Acceptance: rsi/scenarios/239.sh, black box, its own database, a real loop with a fake engine (its header lists checks (0)-(9)):
- (0) the roster takes a pool and the assign a pin;
- (1) refusals, each nothing recorded and the owner unchanged: a running (delivered) task; a pinned task; a task of a paused mission; a cancelled task; a taker in another pool; a taker with no pool; a taker that is not idle (a task delivered; a task assigned only); after (2), the winner as a taker (its task delivered or reported); an idle worker's next task (with the loop stopped: refused, while its second task is taken);
- (2) two `spawn task steal` of one queued task to two idle residents at once: exactly one exits 0, one task.stolen record, the task's session is the winner's; the loser exits 1;
- (3) the moved task is delivered once, into the winner's session (one deliver-<task>- command, its session the winner's; one task.delivered), never into the old worker's;
- (4) B's running task stays B's (delivered, its session, no task.stolen);
- (5) a refold and init-db change no owner and add no record;
- (6) a loop restarted with stealing on: the idle loser takes B's oldest stealable task by itself (task.stolen by spawn-dispatch), one task only (B keeps the next one and its pinned one; the member holding only an assigned task takes nothing);
- (7) that task is delivered once, into the taker's session;
- (8) the paused mission's task and the pinned task never move, and residents outside the pool get nothing;
- (9) `spawn task stages` shows the stage stolen.
On live main 9e1a8d22, 239.sh exits 1 at (0).
